\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The principal neurotransmitters and the receptor families through which they act, with their synaptic actions and the drugs that bind them. ``Type'' distinguishes fast ionotropic channels from slower metabotropic G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs), with the G protein noted where relevant. The receptors drawn on in this section are discussed in the prose above; the remaining subtypes are included to complete the picture. A dash marks a receptor for which no relevant drug is listed.}}{24}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:transmitters}{{1}{24}{The principal neurotransmitters and the receptor families through which they act, with their synaptic actions and the drugs that bind them. ``Type'' distinguishes fast ionotropic channels from slower metabotropic G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs), with the G protein noted where relevant. The receptors drawn on in this section are discussed in the prose above; the remaining subtypes are included to complete the picture. A dash marks a receptor for which no relevant drug is listed}{table.1}{}}
